window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-01-export-ledgers"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-01-export-ledgers"]||{},{"en": "# Economic Thermometer | Exports at $1 Trillion: The Customs Ledger and My Wallet Keep Different Clocks\n\n**Published: November 20, 2026 | Event: November 19 provisional export tally and domestic market close | Verified: November 20, 06:20 KST**\n****\n\n![A 3D conceptual illustration showing the article's central question. Not a photograph of an actual event, product, or person.](images/nov20-01-3d.jpg)\n\n*A 3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph reproducing a real scene or product.*\n\n## The Record Has Arrived; the Settlement Has Not\n\nOne trillion dollars in exports is a big number. But the meaning of the record only comes alive once we confirm exactly what that number counted. The Ministry of Trade and Industry said that as of 2:09:38 PM on November 19, cumulative annual exports had provisionally reached $1 trillion. This is not the final full-year result through December. The correct sentence is not \"this year's exports finished at X\" but \"this year's cumulative performance crossed this threshold.\"[1]\n\nIn the government's account, semiconductors and computers are the main drivers of export growth from expanding global AI investment. It also assessed that the export base has broadened to cosmetics, agri-fishery foods, pharmaceuticals, and small and mid-sized enterprises. Shrinking the achievement to semiconductors alone, or expanding it into a claim that every industry now fares the same, goes beyond the data.[1]\n\n## The Stock Market Moved the Same Day, but It Is Not the Same Statistic\n\nOn the 19th, the KOSPI closed at 5,137.89, up 70.45 points, or 1.39%, from the previous session. The KOSDAQ rose 22.00 points, or 3.32%, to 684.81. The won-dollar rate in the Seoul foreign exchange market stood at 1,330.0 won based on the 3:30 PM daytime session close. All figures are based on Yonhap News' closing reports for the day. The exchange rate must be distinguished from the final value of trading that continues overnight.[2]\n\nExports are the value of goods that crossed a border over a period; stock prices are the market's assessment of future earnings and risks. Just because they were announced on the same day does not mean one export record explains the entire stock price rise. The government's own release also lists risks such as export unit price swings, stronger protectionism, and oversupply.[1]\n\n## Revenue Can Rise While the Paycheck's Temperature Lags\n\nFrom here on, this is analysis. An increase in export value is not the same sentence as an increase in production volume. Products with higher prices can lift the total, and part of what companies earn goes out to raw materials, equipment, interest, and overseas production costs. There are several steps between the customs ledger and a household's real income. So the mere observation that export records and the felt economy differ is no reason to declare either statistic wrong.\n\nThe checklist ahead is simple. Did export value and volume grow together? Did the range of strong items and companies widen? Does it connect to production, employment, and wages? These questions are not conclusions today's data has already answered. They are the criteria for watching how the record spreads. Nor are they a signal to buy or sell any particular sector's stock.\n\n## Today's Clock Table\n\n- Korean exports: the November 19 afternoon provisional cumulative tally for the year.\n- Domestic stock indexes: November 19 regular session closes.\n- Won-dollar exchange rate: the same day's 3:30 PM daytime session close.\n- US regular-session close on November 19: Dow 49,097.06 (-2.19%), S&P 500 7,175.83 (-2.02%), Nasdaq Composite 23,879.20 (-2.25%). Closing tables checked at 06:20 KST on November 20. The US and Korean sessions refer to different trading times.[3]\n\nWelcome a good record, but do not erase the other ledgers. Today's question does not end at \"is the $1 trillion real.\" It continues in watching what costs that achievement passes through and whose life it reaches.\n\n## Sources\n[1] Ministry of Trade and Industry and Policy Briefing, 2026-11-19, provisional cumulative export tally with item- and company-level explanation. https://www.korea.kr/news/policyNewsView.do?newsId=148976090&pWise=main&pWiseMain=A1\n[2] Yonhap News, 2026-11-19, domestic market and exchange rate close. https://www.yna.co.kr/view/AKR20261119149300008?section=economy%2Fall\n[3] Yahoo Finance, 2026-11-19 daily close; Nasdaq COMP. https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/%5EDJI/history/ https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/%5EGSPC/history/ https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/%5EIXIC/history/ https://indexes.nasdaq.com/Index/History/COMP\n"});
