window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-09-okyo-spatial-preview"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-09-okyo-spatial-preview"]||{},{"en": "# Dopamine Lover | Standing Inside the Painting Instead of in Front of It: Maruyama Okyo's Space Experiment\n\n**Published: November 20, 2026 | Tokyo exhibition planned: November 28, 2026 to January 24, 2027 | Verified: November 20, 00:42 KST**\n****\n\n![A 3D conceptual illustration showing the article's central question. Not a photograph of an actual event, product, or person.](images/nov20-09-3d.jpg)\n\n*A 3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph reproducing a real scene or product.*\n\n## How to Read an Exhibition That Has Not Opened\n\nThe Tokyo Metropolitan Edo-Tokyo Museum opens the special exhibition \"Maruyama Okyo: Beyond the Real, a Revolution in Space\" on November 28. According to the official guide, more than 120 works trace his practice from early years to his final period, with Daijoji temple's wall and sliding-door paintings at the center of an inquiry into painting and space. This article is a preview written from the official plan before the opening. It is not a review based on visiting the venue or confirming the actual effect of the installation.[1][2]\n\nOkyo was an Edo-period painter born in 1733 and deceased in 1795. The organizers present him as an artist who valued sketching from observation, and also as one who composed entire spaces beyond the single picture. Read together, the two descriptions show that \"real\" and \"space\" in the exhibition title are not mere modifiers.[1]\n\n## One Painting, and Paintings of Many Rooms\n\nThe official introduction describes the 165-panel set of wall paintings preserved at Daijoji in Hyogo Prefecture as a major achievement of Okyo's art. That is the size of the collection held by the temple. It was not written that all 165 panels travel to this Tokyo exhibition. The exhibition guide says actual wall paintings will be shown alongside recreations of the Peacock Room, the Plantain Room, and the Landscape Room spaces.[1][2]\n\nFrom here on, this is interpretation. A painting in a frame tends to become an object the viewer faces head-on. Paintings that wrap a room make the viewer's position and gaze, and even the experience of moving to the next room, conditions for reading the work. This exhibition's interesting question is the one after \"how lifelike is it\": \"where does it make you stand.\" How persuasively the actual exhibition realizes that experience must be confirmed after the opening.\n\n## One Masterwork Changes the Dates\n\nThe exhibition's full run and individual works' display periods are not the same. In the official work guide, the National Treasure \"Snowy Pines\" folding screens are on limited display from November 28 to December 6. There are also rotations between the first and second halves. Assuming any day within the run shows the same works can break your plan.[2]\n\nThe Tokyo exhibition is announced through January 24, 2027, but the museum is closed from December 25 to January 1. The Monday closure rule has exceptions and additional closed days, so check the museum's latest notice before visiting. This article did not buy tickets or make reservations, and did not verify prices or real-time admission availability.[1]\n\n## When an Old Question of Painting Meets Today's Screens\n\nOne can compare painting's space with today's immersive exhibitions or screen experiences. But there is no need to declare Okyo a forerunner of modern technology, or to merge the purposes of different eras into one. What resembles is the question that a viewer's gaze changes the experience, not the whole of the technology or historical context.\n\nThe viewing stance this preview recommends is simple. Do not only check the works' names; look at what kind of room and gaze each painting presupposed. If you watch how the actual exhibition conveys the feel of thresholds, corners, and distances, you may meet a different story beyond the familiar \"well-painted picture.\" The evaluation of that experience, however, remains ahead.\n\n## Sources\n[1] Special exhibition official site, purpose, Tokyo dates, closed days, artist introduction. https://okyo2026-27.jp/\n[2] Special exhibition official highlights and works guide, display scale, room recreations, individual display periods. https://okyo2026-27.jp/highlight.html"});
