window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-01-producer-prices-two-speeds"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-01-producer-prices-two-speeds"]||{},{"en": "# Global Economy News | Producer prices fell 0.1%. Before taking comfort, separate factories from farms\n\n**Published: November 21, 2026 | Event/data basis: October 2026 statistics, released November 20 | Checked: November 21, 00:45 KST**\n**By: 데일리샷 매크로 리서치**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph of an actual event or person.](images/nov21-01-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event or person. See the article for figures, conditions and sources.*\n\n## A monthly fall and an annual rise\n\nThe Bank of Korea's October Producer Price Index, released on the 20th, fell 0.1% from the previous month. Yet it was 7.2% higher than a year earlier. The first figure measures the change from September; the second measures the difference from a year ago. They do not contradict each other. Reducing this release to either \"inflation is under control\" or \"all prices keep rising\" would miss important information.[1]\n\nPrices moved in different directions even as the overall index fell. Agricultural, forestry and fishery products declined 8.3% month on month, while manufactured products rose 0.3%. Agricultural products fell 11.8% and livestock products 7.3%, but coal and petroleum products rose 1.9% and chemical products 1.5%. This mix gives manufacturers buying raw materials and producers shipping farm goods little reason to feel the same conditions.[1]\n\n## The direction of costs hidden by the average\n\nProducer prices excluding food and energy rose 0.1% month on month and 7.3% year on year. A small fall in the headline index does not mean that the trend excluding volatile items has also turned downward. Services prices were unchanged overall, but restaurant and accommodation services rose 0.7% and transport services 0.6%, while financial and insurance services fell 3.9%. An unchanged average does not mean every industry's prices were frozen.[1]\n\nWhat follows is an interpretation of how to read the indicators. Rather than judging a supplier's request for a price increase from the headline index alone, it is better to start with the categories closest to one's own purchases. A business using substantial agricultural inputs and one using petrochemical feedstocks may have faced different negotiating conditions this month. Individual contract prices also depend on delivery timing, inventory and contract duration, however, so the statistics do not directly determine the right contract price.\n\n## Domestic supply prices and producer prices are not the same index\n\nThe Domestic Supply Price Index rose 0.7% month on month and 8.2% year on year. The Total Output Price Index also rose, by 0.3% and 13.6%, respectively. These increases need to be read alongside the small fall in producer prices. The domestic supply index tracks prices at the domestic supply stage, including imports; the total output index tracks prices of domestic output, including exports.[1]\n\nProducer prices should not be treated as interchangeable with the prices households pay for their shopping. The transaction stages surveyed, product mix and weights differ. These figures alone provide insufficient grounds to predict that consumer prices will move by the same amount next month, or to specify when a particular product's retail price will fall.\n\n## Watch whether the direction spreads in the next release\n\nThe question raised by this release is not simply whether next month's figure will also be negative. It is worth watching whether agricultural, forestry and fishery prices keep falling, whether increases in manufactured products and the index excluding food and energy ease, and whether the directional gap between domestic supply prices and producer prices narrows. These are points for future observation, not forecasts already established by the data.\n\nThe headline decline of 0.1% is a fact. It does not guarantee lower costs for every producer. This month's central issue lies less in the direction of the average than in the different prices that produced it.\n\n\n## Reading with a related video\n\nYonhap News TV reported on Nov 20 that the BOK's October PPI fell 0.1% m/m to 129.66, with farm and fishery products down 8.3% (apples -29.2%, pork -12.8%) while industrial goods rose 0.3% (chemicals +1.5%, coal and petroleum products +1.9%), and restaurant/lodging (+0.7%) and transport (+0.6%) services also climbed — the same item-level divergence behind the small headline decline this article describes.\n\n[Watch on YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cpuoyXofrRk)\n\n## Sources\n[1] Bank of Korea, October 2026 Producer Price Index (preliminary), 2026-11-20. Press release and attached statistics checked. https://www.bok.or.kr/portal/bbs/B0000501/view.do?nttId=11066826&depth=201150&pageUnit=10&pageIndex=1&programType=newsData&menuNo=201264&oldMenuNo=200761\n"});
