DICT["en"]["생산자물가 -0.1%, 안심하기 전에 공장과 밭을 나눠 보자"]="Producer prices fell 0.1%. Before taking comfort, separate factories from farms";
DICT["en"]["한국은행이 20일 발표한 10월 생산자물가지수는 전월보다 0.1% 내렸다. 그러나 지난해 같은 달보다는 7.2% 높다. 앞의 숫자는 9월과 비교한 변화이고 뒤의 숫자는 1년 전과 비교한 수준의 차이다. 두 수치가 서로 모순되는 것은 아니다. 이번 발표를 '물가가 잡혔다' 또는 '모든 가격이 계속 오른다'로 한 줄 처리하면 어느 쪽이든 중요한 정보를 놓친다.[1]"]="The Bank of Korea's October Producer Price Index, released on the 20th, fell 0.1% from the previous month. Yet it was 7.2% higher than a year earlier. The first figure measures the change from September; the second measures the difference from a year ago. They do not contradict each other. Reducing this release to either \"inflation is under control\" or \"all prices keep rising\" would miss important information.[1]";
DICT["en"]["글로벌이코노미뉴스"]="Global Economy News";
DICT["en"]["한 달의 하락과 일 년의 상승"]="A monthly fall and an annual rise";
DICT["en"]["평균이 지운 비용의 방향"]="The direction of costs hidden by the average";
DICT["en"]["국내공급과 생산자가격은 같은 지수가 아니다"]="Domestic supply prices and producer prices are not the same index";
DICT["en"]["다음 발표에서 볼 것은 방향의 확산"]="Watch whether the direction spreads in the next release";
DICT["en"]["출처"]="Sources";
window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-02-remote-compute-export-controls"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-02-remote-compute-export-controls"]||{},{"en": "# Midas's Z-Tech | Even without chips crossing borders, rented computing power comes under scrutiny\n\n**Published: November 21, 2026 | Event/data basis: US rule effective November 19 | Checked: November 21, 00:45 KST**\n**By: 데일리샷 매크로 리서치**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph of an actual event or person.](images/nov21-02-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event or person. See the article for figures, conditions and sources.*\n\n## Questions that do not end with a server's address\n\nThe new advanced-semiconductor rule from the US Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security (BIS) targets transactions that remotely provide computing capacity through data centers in third countries, as well as the physical movement of chips. The detailed rule implementing the policy announced on the 16th was published in the Federal Register on the 19th and took effect that day. The fact that comments are being accepted does not make it merely a proposal without legal effect.[1][2]\n\nThe Federal Register summary explicitly addresses the indirect provision of advanced computing capacity based on US technology to companies in countries of concern. Coverage depends on factors including specified remote computing services, the location of relevant customers' headquarters, and direct or indirect ownership and control. Locating a server overseas is therefore not enough to end the review. Conversely, this does not mean that every cloud service used by a foreign person is prohibited across the board.[2]\n\n## Separate country names from transaction conditions\n\nThe rule also includes licensing requirements for exports, reexports and in-country transfers of advanced semiconductors to Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand and Brazil. It describes an exception, subject to specified reporting conditions, for chips destined for companies headquartered in the United States or whose ultimate parent is headquartered there. Calling all five countries \"semiconductor export-ban countries\" would therefore misrepresent the rule's structure.[2]\n\nCommercial recommendation systems are also presented as an exception to the remote-computing licensing requirements. This does not establish that calling something a recommendation function exempts every transaction. Actual applicability requires checking the item, technology, intended use, customer relationships and conditions in the rule together. This article is neither an export-license determination for any individual company nor legal advice.[2]\n\n## A shared task for foundries and data centers\n\nThe rule also changes the presumptions applying to front-end fabricators and packaging companies making advanced logic chips. The Federal Register explains that the aim is stronger oversight of routes through which design companies in certain destinations use production capacity in third countries. Customer checks extend beyond paperwork at the point of shipment to who designs, who controls and who uses the computing power.[2]\n\nThe following business interpretation should be distinguished from the rule itself. If manufacturers, cloud providers and intermediaries each hold only their own customer lists, gaps may remain in identifying end users. In reviewing transactions, the ability to explain ownership structures, the type and scale of computing supplied and the ultimate purpose, rather than just naming the contracting party, may become important. These are practical questions to check, not a claim that the new rule requires identical documents from every company.\n\n## The unit of regulation is changing\n\nThe rule also formally rescinds the previous AI Diffusion Rule. Because withdrawal and new controls appear together, neither \"easing controls\" nor \"a complete blockade\" adequately describes it.[2]\n\nThe more important change concerns how access is measured. If rented computing power can train models without ownership of the chips, policy cannot stop at ownership and logistics. Technology companies need to examine the commercial relationships connected to a server, not merely a dot marking its location on a map.\n\n\n## Reading with a related video\n\nEdaily TV's Aftermarket (Nov 17) reported that the new US rule blocks Chinese firms' remote use of AI chips, closing the cloud-computing workaround — the same shift this article describes: scrutiny moving from the physical movement of chips to remotely provided computing power through third-country data centers.\n\n[Watch on YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AKYXLK2RPU4)\n\n## Sources\n[1] BIS, announcement of the remote-access rule, 2026-11-16. https://www.bis.gov/press-release/bis-issues-rule-addressing-remote-access-american-super-intelligence-technology\n[2] Federal Register, Addressing Remote Access to Advanced Semiconductors, 2026-11-19. Summary, effective date and exception structure checked. https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/11/19/2026-23529/addressing-remote-access-to-advanced-semiconductors\n"});
