window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-06-china-fta-promise-and-entry"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-06-china-fta-promise-and-entry"]||{},{"en": "# Global Economy News | Substantive conclusion of Korea-China FTA talks: look at the threshold's rules, not just an open door\n\n**Published: November 21, 2026 | Event/data basis: substantive conclusion of follow-up negotiations on November 18 | Checked: November 21, 00:45 KST**\n**By: 데일리샷 매크로 리서치**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph of an actual event or person.](images/nov21-06-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event or person. See the article for figures, conditions and sources.*\n\n## Between announcing an agreement and entry into force\n\nOn the 18th, the government declared the substantive conclusion of follow-up negotiations on services and investment under the Korea-China Free Trade Agreement (FTA). The original agreement took effect in December 2015, and the follow-up talks began in March 2018. The joint government release explains that negotiations have ended but some technical drafting will be completed through working-level consultations. It also states that legal review, formal signing and procedures for entry into force are planned next.[1]\n\nWhat has been confirmed is therefore a negotiated outcome containing specific opening commitments. It does not mean every commitment takes effect today or every permit is processed automatically. Assessing expectations requires setting the announced commitments alongside the systems actually implemented.\n\n## Most-favored-nation treatment is not an unconditional pass\n\nAccording to the government, China promised most-favored-nation treatment no less favorable than that given to third countries in fields including films and games. It also agreed to apply nondiscrimination to prior reviews of content such as online games and films. These commitments may improve the predictability of market access. The release does not say that prior review itself will be abolished or that approval of individual works is guaranteed.[1]\n\nThe parties agreed to expand coproduction, limited to films under the original agreement, to dramas, animation, entertainment programs and documentaries. Producers need to examine both whether the scope for cooperation widens and how the eventual effective text and operating procedures are finalized. The declaration is insufficient evidence that every cultural-sector restriction has immediately been abolished.[1]\n\n## Tourism and finance involve different promises\n\nFor tourism, the outcome includes a commitment allowing Korean travel agencies established in 23 areas of China to arrange overseas travel for Chinese and foreign residents of China. It is not a blanket statement applying to every Korean travel agency established anywhere in China. Legal services, distribution and construction also require reading specific conditions relating to regions or projects.[1]\n\nFor finance, the parties agreed to future most-favored-nation treatment: if China promises wider financial-market opening in a future trade agreement with a third country, Korean companies will also benefit. Turning this financial-sector explanation into a general rule automatically extending every foreign benefit across all other services would change its scope. The detailed reservation schedules and final agreement text also need to be checked.[1]\n\n## Put another clock into the business plan\n\nWhat follows is a business interpretation. Conclusion of negotiations may give companies a basis to seek partners and design products. Revenue projections, however, need separate clocks for signing and entry into force, implementing regulations, and actual permits and contracts. Booking the diplomatic announcement straight into that quarter's performance would miss timing risk.\n\nThis is neither news that nothing has changed nor news that every barrier has disappeared. Its significance lies in promises of comparable treatment and procedures. Future verification means following how consistently those promises work in the final text and in practice.\n\n\n## Reading with a related video\n\nCiting the MOTIE press materials, Ki-jung Kim explains that \"substantial conclusion\" means agreement on key issues, not entry into force, and breaks down the MFN pledge for films and games, non-discrimination in content pre-screening, the widened co-production scope, Korean travel agencies in 23 designated regions arranging overseas trips for local residents, and future MFN in finance. The on-screen figures — goods trade with China of $272.9 billion (20.7%) versus services of $38.8 billion (12.4%) in 2024 — support this article's point that the size of the opened door is in the fine print.\n\n[Watch on YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6wPGqsGiWes)\n\n## Sources\n[1] Joint government release including the Ministry of Trade and Industry, declaration of substantive conclusion of Korea-China FTA follow-up negotiations on services and investment, 2026-11-18. All six pages of the attached PDF checked. https://www.mcst.go.kr/site/s_notice/press/pressView.jsp?pSeq=22856\n"});
