window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-08-borrower-debt-flow-stock"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-08-borrower-debt-flow-stock"]||{},{"en": "# Midas's Z-Tech | New borrowing fell while mortgages grew: two faces of average household debt\n\n**Published: November 21, 2026 | Event/data basis: Q3 2026 statistics, released November 20 | Checked: November 21, 00:45 KST**\n**By: 데일리샷 매크로 리서치**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph of an actual event or person.](images/nov21-08-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event or person. See the article for figures, conditions and sources.*\n\n## KRW 30.28 million and KRW 97.98 million answer different questions\n\nIn the Bank of Korea's preliminary borrower-level household debt statistics for the third quarter, released on the 20th, new household loans per borrower were KRW 30.28 million, down KRW 3.86 million from the previous quarter. Meanwhile, quarter-end outstanding household loans per borrower were KRW 97.98 million, up KRW 80,000. Smaller new borrowing and a larger accumulated balance can coexist because they measure new transactions and outstanding balances.[1]\n\nCalling these figures debt per head of the entire population would also use the wrong denominator. They are borrower-level data using a NICE credit-information sample equivalent to 4.8% of the population aged 18 and older with credit information. Borrowers, households and the entire population are not the same group. The Bank of Korea also explains that survey and compilation standards differ from those of other statistics, including household credit statistics.[1]\n\n## A fall in overall new loans is not a contraction in every product\n\nNew mortgage loans per borrower averaged KRW 214.51 million, up KRW 6.22 million from the previous quarter. The average for all new household loans fell while the mortgage average moved the other way. Quarter-end mortgage balances also increased KRW 1.47 million to KRW 163.4 million per borrower.[1]\n\nThere is nothing inherently odd about the average new mortgage exceeding the average across all new loans. Product sizes and the groups borrowing them differ. Adding or subtracting the two averages cannot calculate a typical household's home-buying cost. These statistics alone also do not establish how much the same individual increased borrowing.\n\n## Do not assign a single cause to a changing average\n\nThe Bank of Korea explains that average new loans declined among borrowers in their 40s, those in Daegu and North Gyeongsang, and those borrowing from nonbank financial institutions. Changes in an average, however, can reflect both actual loan sizes and the changing mix of new borrowers and products. This table alone cannot establish that one particular policy caused the entire decline or that households' repayment capacity improved.[1]\n\nThe notes also explain that some business loans through accounts in individuals' names may be included. The label \"household\" therefore should not be read as a complete list of loans used only for living and housing expenses. The latest figures are preliminary and will be finalized with the following quarter's release.[1]\n\n## Personal borrowing decisions need cash flow, not averages\n\nWhat follows is a general financial decision-making principle. What other people borrow on average is not the right borrowing amount for oneself. Actual repayments, income fluctuations, interest-rate terms, maturity and emergency funds need to be considered together. A lower borrower average does not expand an individual's borrowing capacity, and a higher mortgage average is no reason to buy a bigger home.\n\nThe point of these statistics is not a binary choice between rising and falling debt. Separating new loans from balances, overall loans from mortgages, and averages from borrower composition reveals different changes. In financial reporting, what matters next after the number is always its denominator.\n\n\n## Reading with a related video\n\nA related explainer published by Gyeongje-bokssi on September 7, 2026 uses the BOK’s Q2 2026 borrower-level debt data to explain the distinction between new lending and balances, and between a per-borrower average and all households’ debt. It does not report the Q3 figures released in November. Use this earlier-quarter explanation only as a reading frame; the Q3 values in this article come from the cited BOK release.\n\n[Watch on YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nZDDT8heDOc)\n\n## Sources\n[1] Bank of Korea, Borrower-Level Household Debt Statistics in Q3 2026, 2026-11-20. Full attached PDF, statistical tables and notes on use checked. https://www.bok.or.kr/eng/bbs/E0000634/view.do?menuNo=400069&nttId=11066825\n"});
