window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-10-keynes-enough-and-uncertainty"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-10-keynes-enough-and-uncertainty"]||{},{"en": "# Global Economy News | Why reread Keynes? Before making more, ask what growth is for\n\n**Published: November 21, 2026 | Event/data basis: November 2026 book review; publisher's excerpt of July 24 | Checked: November 21, 00:45 KST**\n**By: 데일리샷 매크로 리서치**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph of an actual event or person.](images/nov21-10-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event or person. See the article for figures, conditions and sources.*\n\n## This is not a review based on reading the whole new book\n\nIn November, EH.Net published Ranjit Dighe's review of Robert Skidelsky's \"Keynes for Our Times.\" This article is an explanation based on that review and the book excerpt Yale University Press published in July. It is not an independent review based on reading the whole book. The reviewer's summary, the author's arguments and this publication's interpretation are distinguished.[1][2]\n\nAccording to Dighe, the book moves through Keynes's philosophy, monetary theory, effective demand and critique of capitalism to present-day problems. The Keynes the reviewer emphasizes cannot be contained within textbook prescriptions for economic stimulus. He is read as someone asking what the economy should be run for, as well as how to make it larger.[1]\n\n## Talking about enough requires talking about distribution\n\nIn the publisher's excerpt, Skidelsky sees economic growth's purpose as providing the resources for everyone to lead a decent life. He also notes that standards of what counts as enough change with technology and society, and that many people even in rich countries have not reached that standard. This is not a simple declaration that growth has become unnecessary.[2]\n\nApplied to today, this argument makes growth in aggregate output and stability in everyday life separate things to check. That is this publication's interpretation. Even when productivity and income rise, the burdens of housing, care and education, and constraints on time, require separate questions. The excerpt alone cannot establish the distribution of income in any particular country or quantify the effects of a policy.\n\n## Forecasting well is different from knowing the future\n\nDighe explains that Keynes's interest in uncertainty carries into an understanding of investment and effective demand. The issue is that companies anxious about the future do not invariably increase investment simply because they have funds. This is the reviewer's interpretation of the book, not a finding that every recession has the same cause.[1]\n\nSkidelsky's excerpt asks a similar question about AI. He challenges the notion that an ability to find past patterns faster guarantees perfect foresight. His imagining of how Keynes might view today's technological competition is a contemporary author's interpretation. It must not be quoted as though Keynes himself actually commented on present-day AI.[2]\n\n## Record growth's purpose alongside its pace\n\nDighe's review also introduces the book's proposals on public investment, employment and international imbalances. These need not be turned directly into a single policy prescription endorsed by this publication. Judging the scale, funding, timing and expected effects of government spending requires separate evidence about current inflation, employment and fiscal conditions.[1]\n\nThe benefit of rereading an economist from history is not copying past sentences as today's answers. It is asking how growth should be measured, who enjoys its results and how to choose in the face of an unknown future. When news about the capacity to produce more is everywhere, the question of what that capacity should be used for deserves a place beside it.\n\n## Related video: sharing productivity gains as income and time\n\nIn a Rethinking Economics interview published by Follow the Money on June 13, 2017, Skidelsky distinguishes taking productivity gains as more income from taking them as leisure (4:51–5:55). He then argues that when gains go to a small minority, many people work long hours simply to maintain their living standards (6:03–6:55). This is an older account of his thinking, not a presentation of the 2026 book or current distribution data. Read alongside the review and excerpt, it suggests asking whose income and time improve, rather than treating growth alone as proof of sufficiency.[3]\n\n[Watch the related interview on YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gxo2eiUpQDY)\n\n## Sources\n[1] EH.Net, Ranjit S. Dighe's review of Keynes for Our Times, November 2026. Full text checked. https://eh.net/book_reviews/keynes-for-our-times/\n[2] Yale University Press, An Excerpt: Keynes for Our Times, 2026-07-24. Full publisher's excerpt checked. https://yalebooks.yale.edu/2026/07/24/an-excerpt-keynes-for-our-times/\n[3] Follow the Money / Rethinking Economics, Robert Skidelsky on Basic Income and Leisure, 2017-06-13. YouTube automatic transcript checked at 4:51–6:55. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gxo2eiUpQDY\n"});
