window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-22-01-bok-november-decision-checklist"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-22-01-bok-november-decision-checklist"]||{},{"en": "# Before the Answer on Rates: Three Things to Read at the November Monetary Policy Board Meeting\n\n**DailyShot Macro Research | Economy Thermometer(Economic Indicators) | November 22, 2026**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event, person or venue, or a historical reconstruction.](images/nov22-01-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration. Not an actual event photograph or historical reconstruction. Dates, conditions and sources are in the article.*\n\n**Event/source basis: October 22 decision; November 26 meeting scheduled**\n\n## The Hold Was Not an Abandonment of Direction\n\nThe Bank of Korea's final Monetary Policy Board meeting of the year is scheduled for Thursday, November 26. The date is confirmed on the BOK's official schedule. The meeting has not yet taken place. As of Sunday the 22nd, neither a hike nor a hold can be written as an outcome.[1]\n\nThe starting point is the October 22 decision statement. The board kept the base rate at 3.00% per annum, with all seven members voting in favor. Yet the same document stated it would assess the timing and pace of any further increases. A unanimous hold in the vote and a policy direction that left tightening open existed at the same time. Reading a single pause as the end of the hiking stance makes it easy to misunderstand the next decision.[2]\n\nThe reasons for the October decision were not a simple economic slowdown either. The BOK pointed to high growth in exports and investment, elevated inflation, and financial-stability risks such as the Seoul metropolitan housing market, while also explaining the need to assess the Middle East situation, uncertainty in major economies' policies, and the ripple effects of the two earlier hikes. That was the judgment at the time; it is no guarantee the November meeting will simply repeat it.[2]\n\n## First, Read the Conditions in the Sentences, Not the Rate\n\nThe first thing to watch at this meeting is the reasoning next to the number. Even if the rate is unchanged, if the assessment of inflation hardens, or if the weight given to waiting longer for the effects of earlier hikes grows, the next policy question changes. Conversely, even if a hike arrives, the pace that follows is not automatically fixed.\n\nThis is not a prediction but a proposal about the order of reading. Compare first which items on growth, inflation, and financial stability changed in the statement, and what conditions attach to the changed explanations. Separating the market's advance expectations from the committee's actual decision is also basic. A Yonhap News outlook for the coming week on the 21st introduced observations of a resumption of hikes, but a preview article is not a resolution.[3]\n\n## Second, Separate the Growth Forecast from the Felt Economy\n\nThe October statement expected this year's and next year's growth rates to exceed the August forecast by a considerable margin, citing the semiconductor cycle and strong exports and investment. At the same time, it noted as an uncertainty the extent to which the semiconductor expansion spills into the domestic economy. A forecast that growth may rise and the question of how broadly that growth reaches households and industries are different questions.[2]\n\nA revised economic outlook is also scheduled for the 26th. It is worth looking not only at whether the numbers are revised up but also at the explanations of consumption, exports, investment, and inflation together. Even if the annual forecast improves, not every household's real income grows by the same amount. That is why the industry-level and income-level spillovers need to be checked in separate data. This article does not pre-fill forecasts that have not yet been released.[3]\n\n## Third, a Dot Plot Is Not a Promise\n\nYonhap News reported that this meeting is scheduled to present a dot plot reflecting members' rate outlooks six months ahead. It is material for reading information about the future path, but it is not a contract that must be kept even when conditions change. Attention belongs on the distribution of the numbers and their premises.[3]\n\nWhat households and companies can prepare also differs from betting on a specific decision all at once. If you first check when the interest rate on your own contracts is reset and your schedule of cash outlays, you can separate when a change in the base rate actually arrives as a cost. This is not a recommendation to take out a particular loan or choose an investment product. It means the time of policy and the time of contracts differ.\n\nThe core this week is not in guessing first whether the rate will rise. It is in reading separately what is already a confirmed judgment and what is still a conditional outlook. The answer from the November 26 meeting must be confirmed after that day's resolution and explanations come out.\n\n## Video to Watch Along\n\nBank of Korea, \"Monetary Policy Direction\" opening remarks, October 22, 2026. Not the November decision video, but material from the immediately preceding meeting for comparison. The returned video transcript was checked against the official statement.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Api3Y-gosT8\n\n## Sources and Scope of Verification\n\n[1] Bank of Korea, official schedule of Monetary Policy Board meetings. Confirmed November 26, 2026. https://www.bok.or.kr/eng/main/contents.do?menuNo=400020\n\n[2] Bank of Korea, Monetary Policy Direction, 2026-10-22. Text of the statement confirmed. https://www.bok.or.kr/portal/bbs/P0000559/view.do?menuNo=200690&nttId=11065823\n\n[3] Yonhap News, The Economy Next Week, 2026-11-21. Report on the scheduled meeting, economic outlook, and dot plot; an advance outlook, not a result. https://www.yna.co.kr/view/AKR20261121014700002\n"});
