window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-22-02-ulsan-ai-infrastructure-bottleneck"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-22-02-ulsan-ai-infrastructure-bottleneck"]||{},{"en": "# The Race After Ordering the GPUs: The Power and the People the Ulsan Data Center Revealed\n\n**DailyShot Macro Research | Factory Manager's Report(Industry & ICT) | November 22, 2026**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event, person or venue, or a historical reconstruction.](images/nov22-02-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration. Not an actual event photograph or historical reconstruction. Dates, conditions and sources are in the article.*\n\n**Event/source basis: November 21 site visit; operation start plan**\n\n## When a Construction-Site Question Becomes Industrial Policy\n\nThe questions that came out of the president's November 21 visit to the SK AI data center in Ulsan did not stop at semiconductor demand. They extended to whether there is a shortage of specialized construction workers, and how the power gap is bridged before emergency generators kick in during an outage. This is why AI infrastructure is hard to assess by announced investment sums or GPU counts alone.[1]\n\nAccording to the Cheong Wa Dae written briefing, the facility is scheduled to begin operation at the end of 2027 and was introduced as the starting point of SK Group's project to build 5GW-scale AI data centers. The site manager gave a progress rate of 71%. Here, 5GW is a figure about the group's business plan, not capacity now running in Ulsan. The progress rate likewise differs from a commercial-operation record in which servers handle customer workloads.[1]\n\nPlanning, construction, equipment delivery, testing, and operation are different states. Skipping between them produces the false story that a policy announcement is already revenue and construction progress is already utilization. This article confirmed the site visit and official announcements; it is not an article that confirmed completion or commercial operation.\n\n## The Economics of Equipment That Bridges Three Seconds\n\nThe official briefing carried the on-site explanation that a UPS, an uninterruptible power supply, can supply power during the roughly three seconds it takes to switch to emergency generators. This is a design and operating explanation offered by site officials. It cannot be stretched to mean that every failure has actually been verified or that uninterrupted operation is permanently guaranteed.[1]\n\nBut this short interval is a good starting point for reading the economics of AI infrastructure. However much you raise the performance of computing equipment, if power, cooling, and the operating system do not work together, the service that can be offered to customers shrinks. So the race to buy equipment and the race to deliver computing stably are not the same. The latter race takes less visible equipment, people, and testing time.\n\nThis part is the editorial desk's industrial interpretation. The on-site briefing alone cannot calculate the facility's profitability or failure rate. What needs to be verified is delivery on the completion schedule, passing of tests, power procurement terms, customer contracts, and actual utilization. Items not yet publicly confirmed must not be filled in with optimistic forecasts.\n\n## Regional Decentralization and Power Competitiveness Must Be Proven Together\n\nThe government explained that region-centered infrastructure investment can be an opportunity for balanced development. The president stressed that a stable, inexpensive supply of renewable-energy power over the long term is a major factor in industrial competitiveness. These are policy directions and expectations. The visit itself is not a confirmed record of lower electricity prices or local job creation.[1][2]\n\nWhat should be asked from a regional perspective is the difference between jobs created during construction and jobs that remain during operation, where the needed specialists will be trained, and which contracts local companies join. Landing one large facility does not mean an automatic recovery of the whole regional economy. To judge results, data on employment and contracts, tax revenue, and energy procurement must follow the investment figure.\n\n## The Next Indicator in the Speed Race\n\nThat a shortage of specialized workers was raised at the November 21 site shows the AI race has bottlenecks outside the chip as well. Whether the country that invests faster is the same as the country that delivers stable service faster must be checked separately.\n\nWhat has been confirmed in Ulsan now is the progress of construction and the issues raised on site. What to watch next is the process by which that leads to the planned operation. Keeping this order lets AI infrastructure be assessed as real industrial production capacity, not a slogan.\n\n## Video to Watch Along\n\nJTBC News video from the Ulsan AI data center site, November 21. The returned transcript contained misrecognitions of names and equipment figures, so exact numbers were based on the official written briefing, not the video's auto-transcript.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gVN3cNJShPw\n\n## Sources and Scope of Verification\n\n[1] Korea Policy Briefing, written briefing on the Ulsan SK AI data center site visit, 2026-11-21. Confirmed the operation schedule, 5GW plan, progress rate, and UPS explanation. https://www.korea.kr/briefing/presidentView.do?newsId=148976223\n\n[2] Yonhap News, Ulsan AI data center site visit, 2026-11-21. Cross-checked on-site remarks and the workforce issue. https://www.yna.co.kr/view/AKR20261121033751001\n"});
DICT["en"]["GPU를 주문한 다음의 경쟁, 울산 데이터센터가 보여준 전력과 사람"]="The Race After Ordering the GPUs: The Power and the People the Ulsan Data Center Revealed";
DICT["en"]["21일 대통령의 울산 SK AI데이터센터 방문에서 나온 질문은 반도체 수요에 그치지 않았다. 건설 전문인력이 부족한지, 정전 때 비상발전기가 켜지기 전 전력 공백은 어떻게 메우는지까지 이어졌다. AI 인프라를 발표된 투자금이나 GPU 수량만으로 평가하기 어려운 이유가 여기에 있다.[1]"]="The questions that came out of the president's November 21 visit to the SK AI data center in Ulsan did not stop at semiconductor demand. They extended to whether there is a shortage of specialized construction workers, and how the power gap is bridged before emergency generators kick in during an outage. This is why AI infrastructure is hard to assess by announced investment sums or GPU counts alone.[1]";
