window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-22-03-ai-strategy-judgment-capacity"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-22-03-ai-strategy-judgment-capacity"]||{},{"en": "# Reports Got Faster. Why Did Decisions Get Slower?\n\n**DailyShot Macro Research | The Chairman's Gamble(Companies) | November 22, 2026**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event, person or venue, or a historical reconstruction.](images/nov22-03-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration. Not an actual event photograph or historical reconstruction. Dates, conditions and sources are in the article.*\n\n**Event/source basis: McKinsey October 15 essay; reintroduced in the November newsletter**\n\n## More Output Is Not More Performance\n\nIf the time it takes to produce an analytical report shrinks, does management get faster too? A McKinsey essay on strategic decision-making published on October 15 points out that this is not necessarily so. Even as data gathering and scenario writing accelerate, the speed at which assumptions are understood, risks are weighed, and execution responsibility is assigned does not accelerate at the same rate. It was reintroduced in the November newsletter, but the original publication date is October.[1]\n\nThe essay explains that as AI accelerates analysis, human judgment can become the new bottleneck. Even when reports multiply, if their sources and assumptions are unknown, executives end up reviewing everything again. Faster production and slower review can appear together inside one company, the argument goes.[1]\n\nThis article is not a statistic announcing the audit results of a specific company or the average return on AI adoption. It is a management perspective McKinsey presented based on observation and cases. It is important not to carry the multiples from an anonymous case over as if they were productivity figures for all companies.\n\n## If Review Time Disappears, Does Accountability Disappear?\n\nThe scope of decisions the essay divides is useful. Repetitive work with clear standards and limits, business judgment under great uncertainty, and high-responsibility decisions such as M&A and restructuring are not candidates for automation at the same speed. Even the former kinds of work, though, need boundaries and error-handling systems set by people.[1]\n\nFrom here on is the editorial desk's interpretation. To reduce the work of executives re-reading entire reports, simply cutting the length of summaries is not enough. Which numbers are facts and which assumptions change the outcome, and what being wrong would force a reversal of the decision, must be placed up front. Reducing the amount to read and reducing the grounds one can take responsibility for are different things.\n\nOne example is a format in which the first page of meeting material carries, side by side, not just the conclusion but the key assumptions, unconfirmed items, and the items the decision-maker must choose. This is not a single prescription proven for every company, but a proposal about where to place the burden of judgment. Different designs are needed depending on the industry and risk level.\n\n## The Training That Disappears When You Cut the Newcomer's Hours\n\nMcKinsey points to one more cost. The work of a junior analyst gathering data and fixing errors in models is simple repetition, and at the same time it was the process of building judgment. If that work is removed wholesale and only finished answers are received, the training opportunities for the people who will later be entrusted with harder decisions can shrink.[1]\n\nSo education can hardly end with tool usage. What is needed is the experience of comparing raw data with results, explaining calculations and assumptions, and fixing wrong outcomes. The fact that a report came out quickly is not evidence that its author understood its contents. The essay proposes structured apprenticeship-style training and the designation of accountable owners.[1]\n\nThis logic is not an argument against using AI. It is about avoiding the contradiction of removing people's opportunities to understand and then handing them more judgment to make. With the same budget, making decisions the organization can review and execute well matters more than producing more material.\n\n## The Board's New Questions\n\nA related interview video presents the view that for AI pilots to lead to real value, the business domain, data, talent, and operating model must change together. The video below is not a reading of the October essay, but a separate interview useful for comparing execution bottlenecks.[2]\n\nWhat executives should ask at the next meeting is not only how many reports were generated. It is who verified the quality of the decision, who is responsible when an error occurs, and what the juniors learned. The speed of analysis is only one part of adoption results, not the company's entire capacity for judgment.\n\n## Video to Watch Along\n\nMcKinsey: Why Agentic AI Pilots Stall. A related interview on the conditions under which AI adoption leads to execution results. Not introduced as the official video version of the essay above.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6FZOBMMJhEU\n\n## Sources and Scope of Verification\n\n[1] McKinsey, The judgment bottleneck: Balancing AI and human speeds in strategy, 2026-10-15. Full original text confirmed; anonymous cases not generalized into overall statistics. https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/the-judgment-bottleneck-balancing-ai-and-human-speeds-in-strategy\n\n[2] Related public interview, McKinsey: Why Agentic AI Pilots Stall. The discussion of combining business domain, data, talent, and operating model confirmed in the returned transcript. Individual revenue multiples in the video are not cited. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6FZOBMMJhEU\n"});
DICT["en"]["보고서는 빨라졌는데 결정은 왜 느려졌나"]="Reports Got Faster. Why Did Decisions Get Slower?";
DICT["en"]["분석 보고서를 만드는 시간이 줄면 경영도 빨라질까. 맥킨지가 10월 15일 공개한 전략 의사결정 기고는 꼭 그렇지 않다고 지적한다. 자료 수집과 시나리오 작성이 빨라져도, 가정을 이해하고 위험을 따지며 실행 책임을 정하는 속도는 같은 비율로 빨라지지 않기 때문이다. 11월 뉴스레터에 다시 소개됐지만 원문 발행일은 10월이다.[1]"]="If the time it takes to produce an analytical report shrinks, does management get faster too? A McKinsey essay on strategic decision-making published on October 15 points out that this is not necessarily so. Even as data gathering and scenario writing accelerate, the speed at which assumptions are understood, risks are weighed, and execution responsibility is assigned does not accelerate at the same rate. It was reintroduced in the November newsletter, but the original publication date is October.[1]";
