window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-22-04-black-friday-total-cost-and-identity"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-22-04-black-friday-total-cost-and-identity"]||{},{"en": "# What to Check Before the 89% Discount: The Real Price of Black Friday\n\n**DailyShot Macro Research | Trend Check Card(Consumption & Retail) | November 22, 2026**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event, person or venue, or a historical reconstruction.](images/nov22-04-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration. Not an actual event photograph or historical reconstruction. Dates, conditions and sources are in the article.*\n\n**Event/source basis: Crackdown November 2 to December 27; 2024 Korea Consumer Agency prevention material**\n\n## The Judgment That You Bought Cheap Is Completed After Delivery\n\nThe discount rate is the biggest number you see before pressing the pay button. But if the goods never arrive or cannot be returned, that number fails to explain the value of the transaction. What to check ahead of this year's cross-border shopping peak is, along with the price tag, the seller's actual identity, how your customs information is used, and the evidence to keep for a dispute.\n\nKorea Customs Service material registered on Consumer24 announces a special crackdown on abuse of overseas direct purchasing from November 2 to December 27. The crackdown targets smuggling disguised as personal use for resale, theft of personal customs clearance codes, illegal food and drug products, and goods infringing intellectual property rights. It means that, apart from an individual's bargain shopping, there are distribution risks that exploit the simplified clearance system.[1]\n\nThe crackdown being underway does not guarantee the quality and refunds of every overseas mall. A customs crackdown and a consumer actually receiving goods from the counterparty to a contract are different procedures. A famous logo or the name of an advertising platform cannot substitute for verifying the seller.\n\n## Do Not Turn Old Damage Cases into This Year's Statistics\n\nMaterial distributed by the Korea Consumer Agency in November 2024 contains cases in which people paid after seeing big-discount ads for famous brands but never received the goods. The discount rates and purchase amounts in those cases are numbers describing the damage at that time. They must not be carried over as if they were damage newly occurring in 2026 or this year's average loss.[2]\n\nThe part of that material that remains useful is the structure of the scheme rather than the recency of the statistics. Addresses similar to official malls, copied images and logos, and the absence of seller information can cloud a consumer's judgment. The linked related video also explains the problem of impersonation malls. The damage counts in the video were not used as this year's total damage scale.[2][3]\n\n## Divide the Cart's Number into Three Lines\n\nThe first line is the actual payment total. Check not only the product price but also shipping, the billing currency, the card issuer's exchange and overseas-use terms, and the applicable taxes and customs costs. You cannot assume the same duty-free standard applies to every direct purchase; it varies by item and shipping conditions. This article does not present a simple table of amounts as a customs determination for all products.\n\nThe second line is the cost of returns. You need to know where the return address is, who pays the shipping, and what the exchange and refund conditions are. The phrase free shipping does not mean free returns. If the seller's contact and terms are unclear, a low price alone hardly offsets the risk.\n\nThe third line is verifiable records. Keeping the ad screen, the seller's address and order details, payment proof, and contact records lets you explain the transaction in a dispute. The Consumer Agency advises that if a cross-border purchase problem is hard to resolve, you can apply for consultation at the International Transaction Consumer Portal. The existence of a consultation channel does not guarantee a refund.[2]\n\n## Customs Information Is Not a Discount Coupon\n\nIt is worth noting that the Korea Customs Service singled out theft of personal customs clearance codes as a separate type in this crackdown. Identification information needed to complete a transaction and information demanded for marketing must be distinguished. Handing over excessive information before the seller's identity is confirmed creates risks beyond the product price.[1]\n\nThis shopping season's question does not end with how much cheaper it got. It is whom you pay how much, under what conditions you receive the goods, and with what records you can explain a problem. The bigger the discount, the better it is not to write these questions small.\n\n## Video to Watch Along\n\nA related video explaining damage from impersonation malls. Not presented as this week's new damage statistics, but reference material showing the difference between advertising and seller verification.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cpfdZ08vvZI\n\n## Sources and Scope of Verification\n\n[1] Consumer24, Korea Customs Service, notice of special crackdown on abuse of overseas direct purchasing, registered 2026-11-04. Crackdown period and types confirmed. https://www.consumer.go.kr/user/ftc/consumer/itnld/1076/selectItnldDetail.do?nttNo=1004577620\n\n[2] Consumer24, Korea Consumer Agency, caution on consumer damage from year-end overseas direct purchasing such as Black Friday, 2024-11-29. Marked as past prevention material. https://www.consumer.go.kr/user/ftc/consumer/cnsmrBBS/740/selectInfoCDMGCSLCDetail.do?cntntsId=00000347&infoId=A1080728\n\n[3] Related video, \"Bought after seeing an SNS ad, couldn't even get a refund. Impersonation mall alert.\" Brand impersonation and seller-information problems confirmed in the transcript. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cpfdZ08vvZI\n"});
