window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-22-08-privacy-leak-second-damage"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-22-08-privacy-leak-second-damage"]||{},{"en": "# A Caller Who Knows Your Loan Is Not Proof of a Real Bank\n\n**DailyShot Macro Research | Midas's Z-Tech(Life & Finance) | November 22, 2026**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event, person or venue, or a historical reconstruction.](images/nov22-08-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration. Not an actual event photograph or historical reconstruction. Dates, conditions and sources are in the article.*\n\n**Event/source basis: October 6 alert; November 6 notice of special-response extension**\n\n## When Leaked Information Becomes the Material of Trust\n\nWhen a stranger states your income and loan limit exactly, trust can form. The Financial Services Commission's October 6 consumer alert warned of exactly that point. Because scammers can combine leaked personal information to approach you like a legitimate loan consultation, you must not judge a contact to be legitimate merely because the caller knows the information.[1]\n\nThe financial authorities later extended the special response period for secondary damage from personal information leaks through year-end. The policy video the FSC posted on November 9 explains the measures as of November 6. This is an article re-reading responses already in force and consumer action guidance, not an announcement of a leak incident newly occurring on the 22nd.[2]\n\nThat guidance explained that at the time, no cases of customer funds being drained through voice phishing or smishing related to this leak incident had been reported. This sentence is not converted into a guarantee that there is still no damage at all as of the 22nd. A tally at a specific point in time and the risk ahead are different things.[2]\n\n## Demands to Send Money Citing Compensation and Protection\n\nThe October consumer alert explains schemes that induce transfers or the installation of malicious apps by leading with compensation for leak damage, low-interest refinancing, or credit-score improvement. In particular, it advises that the financial authorities and financial companies do not demand passwords or authentication numbers by phone or text, nor demand transfers to a specific account citing account protection or damage compensation.[1]\n\nThe key to telling fraud apart is not how much the other side knows, but what they demand. If they say you must repay an existing loan and tell you to send money to an account in an individual's name, or tell you to install an app for a security check, it is important not to finish verifying the facts within the contact channel the other party handed you.\n\nThe FSC advises confirming whether and what was leaked directly through the financial company's official website, app, or main phone number. It means finding the official channel independently, instead of the address in a text message or the number a caller gives you. This is not advice blaming the carelessness of victims; it is a procedure for cutting off familiarity fabricated from already leaked information.[1]\n\n## What Does Safe Block Actually Block?\n\nThe official guidance distinguishes safe blocking for credit transactions, non-face-to-face account opening, and open banking. The targets differ: a function that blocks loans and credit card issuance, a function that blocks non-face-to-face account opening, and a function that blocks open-banking inquiries and transfers. Applying for one cannot be described as blocking all financial fraud.[1]\n\nThe authorities guide application routes through branches of the financial companies you deal with, through the My Accounts at a Glance service, or through bank apps. It is also worth noting that an unblocking procedure may be needed when a necessary transaction arises. Before choosing a specific blocking function, its scope and how to unblock it must be checked in the official guidance; this article does not direct any reader to sign up across the board.[1]\n\nThere are responses on the financial companies' side as well. Dedicated desks for affected customers, stronger abnormal-transaction detection, identity verification for large transfers, and industry-wide information sharing were included in the extension measures. It means security responsibility cannot be replaced by one consumer's caution alone.[2]\n\n## If Damage Occurs, Time Comes Before Verification\n\nIf you have actually been deceived into transferring money and a financial loss has occurred, the FSC guidance says to immediately contact the police at 112 or a financial company call center and request a payment suspension. Seeking consultation and acting to stop funds that have already left carry different weights of time. A payment suspension request does not guarantee recovery of the full amount lost.[1]\n\nThe standard for reading security is not more complicated fear. It is separating the other party's amount of information from their identity, verifying the action demanded, and connecting straight to the official channel if a problem arises. The fact that someone knows my information is not proof that my money can be entrusted to them.\n\n## Video to Watch Along\n\nThe November 6 \"Financial News\" video linked from the FSC's official page. It explains the special-response extension as of that date; it is not an announcement of the damage situation on November 22.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vPEyb2Zb4HY\n\n## Sources and Scope of Verification\n\n[1] Financial Services Commission, caution on damage from recent personal information leaks in the financial sector, 2026-10-06. Confirmed the warning schemes, the scope of safe blocking, and the guidance on 112 and payment suspension through financial companies. https://www.fsc.go.kr/no010101/87882\n\n[2] Financial Services Commission, special response to personal information leaks extended through year-end, content dated 2026-11-06, posted 11-09. https://www.fsc.go.kr/no010107/88103\n"});
DICT["en"]["내 대출을 안다는 전화, 진짜 은행이라는 증거는 아니다"]="A Caller Who Knows Your Loan Is Not Proof of a Real Bank";
DICT["en"]["낯선 사람이 자신의 소득과 대출한도를 정확히 말하면 믿음이 생길 수 있다. 금융위원회의 10월 6일 소비자경보는 바로 그 지점을 경고했다. 유출된 개인정보를 조합하면 사기범도 정상적인 대출 상담처럼 접근할 수 있으므로, 정보를 알고 있다는 이유만으로 정상 연락이라고 판단해서는 안 된다는 것이다.[1]"]="When a stranger states your income and loan limit exactly, trust can form. The Financial Services Commission's October 6 consumer alert warned of exactly that point. Because scammers can combine leaked personal information to approach you like a legitimate loan consultation, you must not judge a contact to be legitimate merely because the caller knows the information.[1]";
