window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-22-10-daeyaseong-alliance-munmu"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-22-10-daeyaseong-alliance-munmu"]||{},{"en": "# How the Tragedy of Daeya Fortress Became an International War\n\n**DailyShot Macro Research | Reading Today Through History | November 22, 2026**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event, person or venue, or a historical reconstruction.](images/nov22-10-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration. Not an actual event photograph or historical reconstruction. Dates, conditions and sources are in the article.*\n\n## From One Family's Deaths to the Map of East Asia\n\nWhen Daeya Fortress fell, Kim Chunchu lost his daughter Gotaso and his son-in-law Kim Pumseok. It was a personal tragedy for the politician who would later become King Taejong Muyeol. At the same time, Silla lost a key stronghold of its western defense. Only by holding these two facts together can one understand why the battle of 642 is so often cited as a starting point of the history that led to the Silla-Tang alliance of 648 and the fall of Baekje in 660.\n\nThe video \"The Battle of Daeya Fortress Drives Kim Chunchu Over the Edge\" from the Hwang Hyun-pil Korean History channel tells the event around Kim Chunchu's anger and his diplomatic choices. If the KBS epic drama \"Munmu\" has made you curious about the relationships among these figures, it is a starting point that asks good questions. But an account in which anger moved diplomacy is different from an account in which anger alone produced an international war. The video's strong judgments about individuals are interpretations to be examined, not historical facts in themselves.\n\nThis article's perspective is this. If the tragedy of Daeya Fortress was the trigger that hurried diplomacy along, the direction of that diplomacy was set by the contest over fortresses and transport routes, the demands of the counterpart state, and Tang's strategy. Follow only one person's emotions and the map disappears; follow only the map and the human wounds that decided the war disappear.\n\n## 1. First, divide the timeline: Eulji Mundeok and Kim Chunchu are not figures of the same war\n\nUnder the big name \"Three Kingdoms period,\" the figures can all look as if they stood on one stage. But the Battle of Salsu, in which Eulji Mundeok defeated the Sui army, was in 612. The fall of Daeya Fortress came 30 years later, in 642. The state Goguryeo faced in 612 was Sui; the counterpart from which Kim Chunchu obtained a promise of military support in 648 was Tang.\n\nEulji Mundeok is a figure for understanding Goguryeo's foreign wars. Queen Seondeok was Silla's monarch at the time Daeya Fortress fell, and King Uija was the Baekje monarch who pressed the offensive against Silla. Kim Chunchu was not yet king; he was a leading Silla politician and diplomatic envoy. He later ascended the throne in 654. King Munmu, Kim Beopmin, was Kim Chunchu's son and succeeded to the throne after his father died in 661.\n\nThe right way to connect the four figures is therefore not to put them all into one battle. It is to look at how the three kingdoms made different choices against the unified empires of the Chinese mainland, Sui and Tang, and how those choices changed across generations.\n\n## 2. Daeya Fortress was a family matter and a border matter at once\n\nDaeya Fortress was Silla's western stronghold, in the area of present-day Hapcheon, South Gyeongsang Province. In 642 King Uija's Baekje pressed its offensive against Silla, and in August of that year a Baekje army led by General Yunchung took Daeya Fortress. Kim Pumseok and Gotaso, who defended the fortress, lost their lives in the process.\n\nThe Kim Yu-sin chapter of the Samguk Sagi records that when Kim Chunchu learned of his daughter's death, he nursed his grievance and went to Goguryeo to ask for reinforcements, and Queen Seondeok permitted it. There is in fact a basis for connecting personal loss with diplomatic action. It cannot be dismissed as later imagination.\n\nCaution is needed, however, before fixing the course of the fortress's fall into a single story. The Jukjuk chapter of the Samguk Sagi recounts that Kim Pumseok seized the wife of Geomil, and that Geomil colluded with the enemy and set fire to the storehouses. The records also differ on how Kim Pumseok's family met its end. The Jukjuk chapter says Kim Pumseok killed his family and then took his own life, while the Baekje Annals write that Yunchung killed Kim Pumseok and his family. Even within the same history book, the transmitted accounts do not agree.\n\nSo the floor of confirmed fact is this: Daeya Fortress fell, and Kim Chunchu's daughter and son-in-law died in the process. When recreating the specific scene of the deaths, one must state which record is being followed. That a drama chose one of several records for a scene does not make it the only confirmed history.\n\n## 3. What the fact that he went to Goguryeo first tells us\n\nKim Chunchu did not think from the start only of joining hands with Tang. The first place he went after the fall of Daeya Fortress was Goguryeo. But military cooperation was not concluded, entangled with the question of returning former Goguryeo territory that Silla had taken.\n\nHere the positions of the three kingdoms diverge. Silla needed reinforcements to stop Baekje's offensive. Goguryeo still had its territorial issue with Silla, and tension with Tang mattered too. That a counterpart is in danger does not mean a state sets aside its own interests.\n\nIn 648 Kim Chunchu went to Tang. Silla's monarch at that time was not Queen Seondeok but Queen Jindeok. Tang had the experience of a failed expedition against Goguryeo in 645 and continued to press for Goguryeo's conquest. Silla's aim of checking Baekje meshed with Tang's aim of pressuring Goguryeo. The Silla-Tang alliance was not formed by Silla's request alone, nor by Tang's goodwill alone.\n\nFor this reason Kim Chunchu's choice becomes an object of evaluation. He drew in strong outside military power to get through an immediate crisis, but military power is not a tool that disappears on its own once cooperation ends. The problem remained that allied states can have different war aims and different designs for the postwar order.\n\n## 4. King Uija and Queen Seondeok should not be read only from their endings\n\nRemembering King Uija only through the final scene, as the king of Baekje's fall, makes the offensive of 642 hard to explain. He came to the throne in 641, and the following year he seized several of Silla's fortresses and pressed it hard. The victory at Daeya Fortress was, from Baekje's standpoint, a military achievement. The defeat of 660 does not erase the superiority that came before it.\n\nNor can King Uija be judged a successful strategist on the strength of the early results alone. The attack of the Silla-Tang combined army, the division of Baekje's ruling class, and the response to the war must be examined together. Explaining a kingdom's fall through a monarch's temperament or a single court anecdote leaves out the structural conditions.\n\nQueen Seondeok, too, was a monarch in the middle of war, too much so to read only through stories of cultural heritage and foresight. The fall of Daeya Fortress, the request for support from neighboring states, and Bidam's rebellion of 647 all overlapped in her reign. To understand Kim Chunchu's diplomacy, it must also be seen at the level of a response by the Silla state carried out under the queen's permission. A leading politician's private affairs must not be equated with the whole conduct of the state.\n\n## 5. How to separate fact, sources, and dramatic construction when watching \"Munmu\"\n\nIn an official announcement on October 14, 2026, KBS introduced \"Munmu\" as a 28-episode epic drama dealing with the life of King Munmu and the turbulent period of the Three Kingdoms, and announced its first broadcast for November 14. This article uses that official introduction as its background. It is not a review that watched individual episodes and judged the historical fidelity of each scene.\n\nWhen watching, it helps to divide three layers. First are parts where the broad flow of years and events is confirmed, such as the fall of Daeya Fortress or the Silla-Tang alliance. Second are parts where the sources diverge, such as the end of Kim Pumseok's family. Third are parts the drama fills in as scenes: private conversations, silences, changes of feeling. Before judging whether a scene differs from fact, one must first ask how specific and how consistent the record itself is.\n\nThe same standard can be applied to video lectures. Moral evaluations of Kim Chunchu are interpretations offered by the historian. Explanations that draw modern regional conflict straight from one seventh-century figure's choice likewise require separate evidence. Connect the past to today's questions, but do not overlay today's regional identities directly onto the borders of the Three Kingdoms.\n\n## A history that sees the alliance's gains and costs together\n\nBaekje fell in 660 and Goguryeo in 668, but that did not mean Silla's and Tang's goals coincided. Tang placed governing bodies such as the Ungjin Commandery on former Baekje territory. The clash between Silla and Tang that followed is an important background for understanding King Munmu's era. Closing the war's ending with Baekje's fall alone hides the next problem faced by the drama's title character.\n\nHaving come this far from Daeya Fortress, the question changes too. Beyond how angry Kim Chunchu was, one comes to ask who sets the postwar rules in an alliance made to get through a crisis. At the same time, one should be wary of judging the choices of the states divided in that era by presuming today's single nation-state.\n\nWhat this article brings from the Three Kingdoms period to today is not an answer fixing any figure as a hero or a traitor. It is a way of reading that separates a leader's wound, the pressure of borders, and a great power's calculations, and then connects them again. A war may begin with someone's anger, but it is not explained by that anger alone.\n\n## Related video\n\nHwang Hyun-pil Korean History, \"The Battle of Daeya Fortress Drives Kim Chunchu Over the Edge.\"\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xQAxl-MSLGM\n\nThe video's framing of the question served as this article's starting point, but its value judgments about individuals were not adopted as they stand. Instead of direct quotation, the content was summarized and checked against public historical materials and translated historical sources.\n\n## Sources\n\n1. National Institute of Korean History, Korean Ancient Sources DB, Samguk Sagi, Kim Yu-sin chapter, Kim Chunchu's visit to Goguryeo, with modern rendering and notes\nhttps://db.history.go.kr/ancient/level.do?levelId=sg_041r_0020_0130\n2. National Institute of Korean History, Korean Ancient Sources DB, Samguk Sagi, Jukjuk chapter, the Battle of Daeya Fortress\nhttps://db.history.go.kr/id/sg_047r_0130_0030\n3. Academy of Korean Studies, Battle of Daeya Fortress\nhttps://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0014594\n4. Academy of Korean Studies, King Taejong Muyeol\nhttps://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0059042\n5. Academy of Korean Studies, Eulji Mundeok\nhttps://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0042969\n6. Academy of Korean Studies, Queen Seondeok\nhttps://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0028652\n7. Academy of Korean Studies, King Uija\nhttps://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0043357\n8. National Institute of Korean History, Uriyeoksanet, Ungjin Commandery\nhttps://contents.history.go.kr/mobile/kc/view.do?code=kc_age_10&levelId=kc_o100300\n9. KBS News, October 14, 2026, \"KBS Epic Drama 'Munmu'... First Broadcast on the 14th of Next Month\"\nhttps://news.kbs.co.kr/news/mobile/view/view.do?ncd=8685756\n\n10. Academy of Korean Studies, King Munmu (detailed text)\nhttps://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0019473\n11. National Institute of Korean History, Korean Ancient Sources DB, note on King Munmu in the Samguk Sagi Jikgwanji\nhttps://db.history.go.kr/id/sg_038r_0020_1880\n"});
